Problem Statement :
A trading floor has n stalls, each initially selling a product at a given price. Over time, stall prices are updated, and analysts issue two kinds of queries: one asking how many stalls currently have a price at or below some threshold, and another asking what the k-th cheapest current price is among all n stalls.

Formally, maintain an array of n prices (indexed 1 to n) and process q operations, each one of:

U i v — Update the price of stall i to the new value v.
C X — Output the number of stalls whose current price is ≤ X.
K k — Output the k-th smallest price (1-indexed) among the current prices of all n stalls. It is guaranteed 1 ≤ k ≤ n.
Note that the number of stalls stays fixed at n throughout — updates only change a stall's price, they never add or remove stalls.

Input Format
Line 1: an integer n.

Line 2: n integers — the initial prices.

Line 3: an integer q.

Next q lines: one operation each, in the format U i v, C X, or K k.

Output Format
For every C and K operation (in order), print one line containing the requested integer.

Constraints
1 ≤ n ≤ 2 × 10^5
1 ≤ q ≤ 2 × 10^5
1 ≤ price, v, X ≤ 10^9
1 ≤ i ≤ n, 1 ≤ k ≤ n
Time Limit: 1 secMemory Limit: 256 MB
The output is unique.
Sample Testcase 0
Testcase Input
5
3 1 4 1 5
5
C 3
K 3
U 2 10
C 3
K 2
Testcase Output
3
3
2
3
Explanation
Initial prices: [3,1,4,1,5]. C 3 counts prices ≤3: {3,1,1} → 3. K 3 on sorted [1,1,3,4,5] gives the 3rd smallest, 3. U 2 10 changes stall 2's price from 1 to 10, giving [3,10,4,1,5]. C 3 now counts {3,1} → 2. K 2 on sorted [1,3,4,5,10] gives the 2nd smallest, 3.

